			<div class="content" id="post-content-3570660">
									

<p>Image is copyrighted by Josy Conklin and used with her permission. Source <a href="https://newprovidencedailyphoto.wordpress.com/2012/07/28/turtle-back-rock/">here.</a></p>
<p>I posted this a week ago but have made some pretty major revisions since then. Thanks to everyone who reviewed both rounds of this article. Thanks especially to Modern_Erasmus for helping me understand some problems in the previous version and ARD for helping me work through this version.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I deleted the last version of this article despite its positive rating because I realized I wasn't hitting the points I'd wanted to as well as I could. The article's idea was born in a fit of brainstorming skip ideas inspired by the aesthetic and themes of the anime <em>Mushishi</em> (which is bomb and you should definitely check it out if you haven't), and so a big part of the original idea was a neutral relationship between humanity and relationship that didn't have human values attached to it. However, because I'm totally ripping off the last episode of Mushishi season 2 (and also the lion turtles from ATLA), I felt I needed something more.</p>
<p>The idea of a turtle which had, stored in its memory, something like a story of the entire Pacific led me down a rabbit hole of reading into storytelling's relationship in Hawaiian society-a ton of information that I flat-out did not include in the first draft of this article. For the second draft, I cut a lot of the direct depictions of Moses' family and that conflict and instead only alluded to it; in its place I decided to hone this article's focus on storytelling by actually depicting, in some detail, the culture and society in which this storytelling takes place. Hope you all enjoy reading!</p>
</div>
